{"id": "ddf17592-769b-4e4f-83f1-81f8703a64ac", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll audit this across your connected AWS accounts. I'll cover which IAM users can invoke Bedrock models, including access through groups, attached policies and inline policies. I'll also cover their access keys: age, last use and status. Then I'll rank what to fix first.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_sSsAhViTAeVhr2J0IMSq1s", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "bedrock-iam-audit", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "{
  "task": "Produce a READ-ONLY security audit of which IAM users, and which of their long-lived IAM access keys, can invoke Amazon Bedrock models in every AWS account connected to this agent space (use list_associations / get_agent_space to enumerate accounts; agent space account is 123456789012, region us-east-1). Do not make any mutating calls.",
  "context": "The customer wants a full report and a prioritized 'fix first' list. Focus on IAM users (not roles) and their long-lived access keys (AKIA... keys). Note that IAM is global, so query IAM once per account (use us-east-1 for the call). Also check Bedrock-related account context where cheap (e.g. bedrock list_foundation_models / model invocation logging config via get_model_invocation_logging_configuration in us-east-1 and one other region if relevant) to say whether invocations are logged.",
  "resources": ["All associated AWS accounts, including 123456789012"],
  "criteria": [
    "For each account: list all IAM users (list_users) with PasswordLastUsed, console access (get_login_profile), MFA devices (list_mfa_devices), group memberships (list_groups_for_user), attached managed policies and inline policies for the user AND their groups.",
    "Determine effective ability to invoke Bedrock models: prefer iam simulate_principal_policy for each user against bedrock:InvokeModel, bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream, bedrock:Converse, bedrock:ConverseStream (resource '*') and also bedrock:CreateModelInvocationJob / bedrock:StartAsyncInvoke if feasible. If simulation is not permitted, analyze policy documents manually (get_policy_version, get_user_policy, get_group_policy) looking for bedrock:*, bedrock:Invoke*, AmazonBedrockFullAccess, AdministratorAccess, PowerUserAccess, '*:*', and note any Deny statements, permission boundaries, and SCP/resource limitations you can see. Distinguish: explicit Bedrock grant vs. via admin/wildcard vs. scoped to specific models/resources.",
    "For each Bedrock-capable user list access keys (list_access_keys): key id (last 4 chars is enough plus full AKIA id is fine), status, CreateDate/age in days vs 2026-11-20, get_access_key_last_used (date, service, region), and flag keys that are active and >90 days old, never used, unused >90 days, last used on bedrock, or users with 2 active keys.",
    "Flag extra risk: users with console password but no MFA, users with Bedrock access AND active long-lived keys, users with admin/wildcard access, unused users, Bedrock permissions with resource '*' and no condition, users whose keys were used recently from Bedrock service.",
    "Check IAM credential report (generate_credential_report/get_credential_report) if it helps cross-check key rotation and last-used data.",
    "Return a structured result: per-account summary counts; a table of Bedrock-capable users (user, how access is granted, scope, MFA/console, keys with age/status/last used/last-used service); a prioritized remediation list (P0/P1/P2) with concrete reasons and the specific user/key for each; and clearly state any accounts or calls that failed due to missing permissions or limitations so gaps are explicit. Be concise but complete in the table data."
  ]
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Audit IAM users and access keys able to invoke Bedrock"}], "created_at": null}

